# YourPHR environment configuration (#96, #474).
#
# START WITH THE TEMPLATE FOR YOUR DEPLOYMENT — what must be set before first start differs by
# deployment, so there is one file per kind rather than one file with everything commented out:
#
#   .env.docker.example      docker / docker-compose  — nothing required, defaults are correct
#   .env.baremetal.example   bare metal / VM          — the shipped defaults are CONTAINER paths
#   .env.k8s.example         Kubernetes               — reference list for the pod spec `env:`
#   .env.dev.example         local development        — `make serve-backend`
#
#   cp .env.docker.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored; these templates are committed. NEVER put a real secret in a template.
#
# This file is the shared reference: how the layering works, and the settings whose explanation is
# the same everywhere. See docs/configuration-system.md for the full model.

# --- HOW CONFIGURATION LAYERS ---
#
# Lowest to highest precedence:
#
#   1. shipped defaults      backend/pkg/config/app-default-config.json (the catalogue of every
#                            setting that exists — if it is not in there, it is not a setting)
#   2. .env, then .env_custom
#   3. instance overrides    <data>/config/app-custom-config.json, written by Admin -> Configuration
#   4. real environment      YOURPHR_* variables
#
# So a value set in the real environment cannot be changed from the Admin screen: that screen shows
# it as governed by the environment and refuses to write it, rather than accepting an edit that
# would silently revert on the next restart.
#
# Naming: config key -> env var is the key upper-cased with the YOURPHR_ prefix, with "." and "-"
# becoming "_". `cda_converter.enabled` -> `YOURPHR_CDA_CONVERTER_ENABLED`. FASTEN_* and unprefixed
# names are ignored. At startup the app WARNS about any YOURPHR_ variable that maps to no setting —
# check the log if something you set appears to do nothing.
#
# WHERE the dotenv files are read from: the process WORKING DIRECTORY, in the order .env_custom then
# .env, and neither overrides a value already in the real environment.
#   - local dev (run from the repo root)      -> repo root
#   - Docker / k8s (WORKDIR is /opt/fasten/)  -> mount .env_custom at /opt/fasten/.env_custom
# Both are optional; defaults plus YOURPHR_* env are enough. The flux deployment uses no dotenv file.
#
# DOCKER COMPOSE — IMPORTANT (#397): `docker compose` reads .env only to substitute ${...} inside
# docker-compose.yml. It does NOT forward those values into the container. The shipped compose files
# declare `env_file:` so YOURPHR_* settings reach the app. A docker-compose.yml from before v1.13.4
# lacks that block, and everything below is silently ignored — no warning, the app just runs on
# defaults. Verify with:
#
#   docker compose config | grep YOURPHR_
#
# If your variables are not listed there, the app will not see them.

# --- PATHS ---
#
# The instance data root (#451): the ONE directory holding everything this instance owns and must
# not lose — the config store, the auto-generated signing key, the cache, backups. This is the
# directory to mount as a volume and to back up.
# YOURPHR_STORAGE_DATA_DIR=/opt/fasten/db
#
# The database path is INDEPENDENT and does not derive from the data root. Setting only the data
# root leaves the database at its default path — a derivation once existed and was removed after it
# relocated live databases on upgrade (v1.21.0). Set both, explicitly.
# YOURPHR_DATABASE_LOCATION=/opt/fasten/db/fasten.db
#
# The cache is also independent.
# YOURPHR_CACHE_LOCATION=/opt/fasten/db/fasten.cache.db

# --- SECURITY ---
#
# JWT signing key. Leave UNSET: a strong key is generated and persisted at
# <data root>/.jwt_issuer_key on first start (#102). Set it only to supply your own.
#   generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# YOURPHR_JWT_ISSUER_KEY=
#
# Database encryption is ON by default. The key has no default and must be supplied on every start.
# Enabling encryption currently DISABLES backup and restore (#367).
# YOURPHR_DATABASE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# YOURPHR_DATABASE_ENCRYPTION_ENABLED=true
#
# Secrets print as [REDACTED] in logs and API responses. Turn off only to debug a specific failure,
# and turn it back on — the app warns on every start while it is off.
# YOURPHR_LOG_REDACT_SECRETS=true

# --- LABELS AND LOGGING ---
#
# Shown in the UI footer as "<name>-<semver>" (e.g. demo-1.18.2). Same image everywhere, so set this
# per environment or demo is labelled "prod".
# YOURPHR_WEB_ENVIRONMENT_NAME=demo
#
# DEBUG | INFO | WARN | ERROR
# YOURPHR_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# --- C-CDA / CCD (XML) IMPORT (#254, #735) ---
#
# NOT environment variables in v3. The converter's address and switch are ordinary settings on
# Admin -> Configuration (yourphr.cda-converter.url / .enabled / .timeout-seconds), read on every
# upload, so no restart. The v2 variables YOURPHR_CDA_CONVERTER_ENABLED / _URL / _TIMEOUT_SECONDS
# are not read. Setup, including running the converter itself: docs/import/c-cda.md

# --- SMART ON FHIR OAUTH RELAY (#50, #399) ---
#
# Runtime-configurable — no rebuild to point at your own. The exact callback URL your instance uses
# is reported by GET /api/secure/source/relay-config.
#
# TWO distinct URLs, because they are reached by different parties:
#   YOURPHR_RELAY_URL        where the BACKEND polls /pending. May be private or cluster-internal.
#   YOURPHR_RELAY_PUBLIC_URL the public origin the PROVIDER redirects the user's browser to.
#                            <this>/callback is the OAuth redirect_uri you register with each FHIR
#                            vendor, and it must match EXACTLY.
#
# With a single publicly-reachable https relay, set only YOURPHR_RELAY_URL — the public URL defaults
# to it. Set the public URL when the poll URL is internal or non-https. Unset both to use the
# project's dev/demo relay (https://relay.nerdsbythehour.com).
# YOURPHR_RELAY_URL=https://relay.example.org
# YOURPHR_RELAY_PUBLIC_URL=https://relay.example.org
# Shared secret gating /pending — must match the value configured on your relay.
# YOURPHR_RELAY_SECRET=

# --- EPIC SANDBOX (reference only — NOT read from the environment) ---
#
# YourPHR is bring-your-own client_id: these are entered in the "Connect a SMART source" UI (the
# "Use Epic Sandbox" button pre-fills them). Listed here as a copy-paste reference.
# Full walkthrough: docs/EHR-vendors/epic-sandbox.md
#
#   FHIR base URL : https://fhir.epic.com/interconnect-fhir-oauth/api/FHIR/R4
#   Scopes        : launch/patient patient/*.read openid fhirUser offline_access
#   Redirect URI  : <your relay>/callback   (register this with Epic)
#   Client ID     : your own Non-Production Client ID from https://fhir.epic.com
